5. Data Augmentation

Data augmentation is a technique used to artificially increase the size and diversity of a training dataset by applying random transformations to existing images. Instead of collecting thousands of new images, we create new variations of the existing ones. These transformations include rotation, horizontal flipping, cropping, zooming, brightness adjustment, and color changes. Data augmentation helps reduce overfitting because the model learns to recognize objects under different conditions rather than memorizing specific images.

Example: If the original training image contains a cat, rotating the image by 15° or flipping it horizontally still produces a valid cat image. The CNN learns that the object remains the same even though its appearance has changed slightly.

6. Why Data Augmentation is Important

Without data augmentation, a CNN may memorize the training images instead of learning the actual features of the object. This leads to poor performance on unseen images. By exposing the model to many variations of the same object, augmentation improves the model's ability to generalize to real-world scenarios where images may have different lighting conditions, camera angles, or backgrounds.

Example: A self-driving car trained only on sunny-day images may fail at night. By augmenting images with different brightness levels, shadows, and weather conditions, the model becomes more reliable.

7. torchvision

torchvision is an official PyTorch library developed specifically for Computer Vision tasks. It provides ready-to-use datasets, image preprocessing functions, pretrained CNN models, and utilities for loading and transforming images. Instead of writing preprocessing code manually, developers use torchvision to simplify image loading and preprocessing. It is one of the most commonly used libraries in PyTorch-based Computer Vision projects.

Example: Using torchvision.transforms, we can resize an image, convert it into a tensor, and normalize it using just a few lines of code.

8. transforms.Compose()

Compose() combines multiple preprocessing operations into a single pipeline. Instead of applying transformations one by one, Compose executes them sequentially in the specified order. This makes preprocessing more organized, reusable, and easier to maintain.

Example:

transform = transforms.Compose([
    transforms.Resize((224,224)),
    transforms.ToTensor(),
    transforms.Normalize(
        mean=[0.485,0.456,0.406],
        std=[0.229,0.224,0.225]
    )
])

Here, the image is first resized, then converted into a tensor, and finally normalized before being passed to the CNN.

